-- test-isolation: database-per-test
--
-- ↑ STATBUS-274. This test flips the WHOLE database read-only to prove the
-- guard, so sharing a database with anything else means wrecking it. Before
-- this marker existed, isolation could only be had by taking a 4xx/5xx
-- filename — which would also have dropped the test out of the fast tier,
-- where a 19-millisecond gate belongs. So it ran shared, and the cleanup below
-- was the only thing standing between a failure here and every later test in
-- the run failing too.
--
-- THE CLEANUP STAYS. A test should leave the world as it found it whether or
-- not anyone else is watching. What changed is the STAKES, not the hygiene: a
-- cleanup failure can now only affect this test's own database.
--
-- STATBUS-265: the worker's crash recovery is exempt from the upgrade's
-- read-only accident-guard — and the guard still bites everything else.
--
-- WHY THIS TEST EXISTS. During an upgrade the database default is flipped
-- read-only (ALTER DATABASE ... SET default_transaction_read_only = on) so
-- external writes FAIL rather than land in a window a rollback would discard.
-- Norway (STATBUS-262) showed the cost of that guard catching the wrong thing:
-- the worker's startup crash recovery was refused inside the window, was never
-- retried, and four derive children stayed 'processing' for a week behind a
-- worker that looked perfectly healthy.
--
-- The worker now exempts that ONE call and restores the guard immediately after.
-- This pins the DATABASE semantics that exemption depends on. Four arms, and the
-- fourth is the one most easily skipped:
--
--   1. under the guard, an ordinary write is REFUSED
--   2. under the exemption, the REAL recovery function succeeds and does its job
--   3. RESET restores the inherited guard
--   4. the very next ordinary write is REFUSED again  ← the no-leak arm
--
-- Arm 4 is what stops the exemption from becoming a hole. The worker shares one
-- connection between this recovery and the queue processing that follows, and
-- processing tasks IS user work — precisely what the guard exists to block. An
-- exemption that leaked would defeat the guard entirely while looking fine.
--
-- BOUNDARY, stated so nobody reads more into a green than it carries: this pins
-- the DATABASE-side semantics only. That the Crystal worker places its SET and
-- RESET correctly around the call is covered by type-check and review until
-- STATBUS-270 revives the worker's own test suite.
--
-- WHY THE RECONNECT. The guard's real shape is an INHERITED setting, and a
-- session only inherits at connect time — verified while building this: a
-- mid-session ALTER DATABASE does not change the current session, and RESET
-- restores the value the session STARTED with. Simulating the guard with a
-- session-level SET therefore proves nothing about arm 3, which is exactly the
-- artefact that misled the first attempt at this proof. So the test sets the
-- database default and reconnects to inherit it for real.
--
-- The reconnect is quiet because the test database's name carries the runner's
-- pid (test_094_…_$$ under the isolation this file now asks for; test_shared_$$
-- before it did), and psql's connection banner would put that varying name
-- into the expected output — a test that fails on a different day for no reason.
-- pg_regress already runs psql with -q, so this is belt-and-braces rather than
-- strictly required; it is set explicitly so the file does not silently depend
-- on a harness flag, and it is never set back off, because turning it off makes
-- psql echo command tags that no other expected file in this suite carries.
-- Nothing here is asserted through a command tag: every arm is proved by a
-- SELECT of the actual setting or row state.
\set QUIET on
-- ON_ERROR_STOP STAYS OFF FOR THE WHOLE FILE, DELIBERATELY, AND THIS IS NOT THE
-- USUAL off/on SANDWICH THE OTHER TESTS USE.
--
-- This test runs against the SHARED cloned database, and between ARM 0 and
-- CLEANUP that database is read-only for every session that connects to it.
-- psql has no `finally`. With ON_ERROR_STOP on, ONE unexpected error anywhere in
-- the guarded region aborts the file before cleanup, the ALTER DATABASE survives,
-- and every test that runs after this one in the same run fails on writes it has
-- every right to make — one real failure reported as a dozen fake ones, with the
-- true cause several tests upstream.
--
-- Off costs nothing in rigour: pg_regress judges a test by DIFFING this output
-- against the expected file, not by psql's exit status. An unexpected error still
-- prints, still diverges, still fails the test — and cleanup still runs. It is set
-- explicitly rather than left to the default so the property is stated where it is
-- relied upon.
--
-- THE ONE COST, AND IT FALLS ON A HUMAN, NOT ON THE MECHANISM: if this file's
-- expected output ever changes, READ THE DIFF — an unexpected error appears here
-- as ordinary output, so regenerating the expected file without reading it would
-- baseline a real failure as the new truth.
\set ON_ERROR_STOP off
\echo '=== ARM 0: establish the guard the way an upgrade does, then inherit it ==='
=== ARM 0: establish the guard the way an upgrade does, then inherit it ===
-- ALTER DATABASE needs a literal identifier, and the test database's name is
-- generated per run, so it is resolved through format().
DO $do$
BEGIN
    EXECUTE format('ALTER DATABASE %I SET default_transaction_read_only = on', current_database());
END
$do$;
\c
SELECT current_setting('default_transaction_read_only') AS guard_inherited_by_new_session;
 guard_inherited_by_new_session 
--------------------------------
 on
(1 row)

\echo '=== ARM 1: under the guard, an ordinary write is REFUSED ==='
=== ARM 1: under the guard, an ordinary write is REFUSED ===
-- Transparent refusal: the real error text lands in the expected output, so a
-- change in WHICH error fires is visible rather than swallowed. No DO-block
-- exception handling — that would hide the very thing being asserted.
-- expected to fail with read_only_sql_transaction (25006) — that refusal IS the
-- assertion; a green here without this error means the guard stopped working.
CREATE TEMP TABLE guard_probe (x int);
ERROR:  cannot execute CREATE TABLE in a read-only transaction
\echo '=== ARM 2: under the exemption, the REAL recovery function runs and works ==='
=== ARM 2: under the exemption, the REAL recovery function runs and works ===
SET default_transaction_read_only = off;
SELECT current_setting('default_transaction_read_only') AS exempted;
 exempted 
----------
 off
(1 row)

BEGIN;
-- A task abandoned in 'processing' by a killed worker — the exact state Norway
-- was wedged in. Everything in this transaction is rolled back, so the function's
-- own side effects cannot leak into later tests.
INSERT INTO worker.tasks (id, command, state, worker_pid, process_start_at)
VALUES (999265001, 'derive_statistical_history_facet_period', 'processing', 424242, now());
SELECT state AS seeded_state FROM worker.tasks WHERE id = 999265001;
 seeded_state 
--------------
 processing
(1 row)

-- The real function the worker calls at startup. Its SELECT ... FOR UPDATE is
-- what the read-only window refused on Norway.
--
-- `>= 1`, not `= 1`: the shared database may carry 'processing' rows left by
-- tests that ran earlier, so the exact count is not ours to predict. What this
-- test owns is the row it seeded, and the assertion that matters is the next
-- one — that THAT row is now 'interrupted'.
SELECT worker.reset_abandoned_processing_tasks() >= 1 AS reset_reported_at_least_one;
 reset_reported_at_least_one 
-----------------------------
 t
(1 row)

-- And it did the job: the abandoned row is reclaimable again.
SELECT state AS state_after_reset FROM worker.tasks WHERE id = 999265001;
 state_after_reset 
-------------------
 interrupted
(1 row)

ROLLBACK;
\echo '=== ARM 3: RESET restores the inherited guard ==='
=== ARM 3: RESET restores the inherited guard ===
RESET default_transaction_read_only;
SELECT current_setting('default_transaction_read_only') AS guard_restored;
 guard_restored 
----------------
 on
(1 row)

\echo '=== ARM 4: the very next ordinary write is REFUSED again (no leak) ==='
=== ARM 4: the very next ordinary write is REFUSED again (no leak) ===
-- expected to fail with read_only_sql_transaction (25006), exactly as in ARM 1.
-- If this one SUCCEEDS, the exemption leaked past the call it was carved for and
-- the worker would go on to process user tasks inside the upgrade window.
CREATE TEMP TABLE guard_probe_2 (x int);
ERROR:  cannot execute CREATE TABLE in a read-only transaction
\echo '=== CLEANUP: drop the guard and return the session to normal ==='
=== CLEANUP: drop the guard and return the session to normal ===
-- The exemption is needed once more to run the reverting DDL, then the database
-- default is cleared and a fresh session picks that up.
SET default_transaction_read_only = off;
DO $do$
BEGIN
    EXECUTE format('ALTER DATABASE %I RESET default_transaction_read_only', current_database());
END
$do$;
\c
SELECT current_setting('default_transaction_read_only') AS guard_cleared;
 guard_cleared 
---------------
 off
(1 row)

